WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.177
Before writing RAG code, look carefully at the structure of a source document.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:08.537
The text field contains the knowledge we want to search.

00:00:08.537 --> 00:00:11.762
But the surrounding metadata is just as important.

00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:15.112
A document ID gives us a stable identifier.

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:20.952
The title gives us something readable to display to a user.

00:00:20.952 --> 00:00:21.262
Topic can be used for filtering.

00:00:21.262 --> 00:00:26.977
The updated date helps us reason about freshness.

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:28.672
The source field supports citations.

00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:30.567
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:34.947
In a production system, you might also keep department, language, document type, tenant, access role,

00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:41.612
or page number. A common mistake is to store only the text and throw away everything else.

00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:45.972
That makes the system harder to debug and harder to secure.

00:00:45.972 --> 00:00:50.787
So from the beginning, think of RAG data as text plus metadata.

00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:57.007
The embedding represents the meaning of the text, while the metadata helps us control and explain retrieval.
